<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lotus Sutra</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saddharmapundarika Sutra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lotus Sutra is one of the most influential scriptures of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a>, composed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> around the 1st century CE. It teaches that all beings can attain Buddhahood and that the Buddha uses skilful means to lead them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumarajiva">Kumarajiva</a>'s Chinese translation of 406 became the standard version in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, and the sutra is the basis of the Tiantai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tendai">Tendai</a> schools and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nichiren">Nichiren</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lotus-sutra.jpg" alt="Lotus Sutra" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Text inscribed by Sugawara Mitsushige · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sugawara_Mitsushige_Lotus_Sutra,_01crop.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> title, Saddharma Puṇḍarīka Sūtra, means the Discourse on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-lotus">White Lotus</a> of the True Doctrine. The sutra is presented as a drama of mythological scenes, with the whole cosmos as its stage, and it speaks almost exclusively in images. In the opening chapter the Buddha, at a gathering on Vulture Peak, enters deep meditation and sends a ray of light from between his eyebrows across thousands of Buddha-fields. Mañjuśrī says that the Buddha is about to expound his ultimate teaching.</p><p>Two teachings have been most influential. The first is the One Vehicle, which holds that all Buddhist paths and practices are skilful means leading to Buddhahood. In chapter 2 the Buddha says that the three vehicles are only such means, and five thousand monks leave rather than hear it. The second is that all beings can become Buddhas, so that, in the sutra's words, of any who hear the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a> none will fail to attain Buddhahood. Even <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devadatta">Devadatta</a>, the great Buddhist villain, receives a prediction of Buddhahood. The sutra also teaches that the Buddha's lifespan is immeasurable and that he achieved Buddhahood innumerable ages ago, so that his apparent death was a skilful means.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Scholars generally hold that the text took shape in phases, with chapters 2 to 9 forming the earliest layer, followed by chapter 1 and further chapters. Its authors are unknown, but Donald Lopez judges them to have been highly educated Buddhist monks. Peter Alan Roberts suggests that it may have arisen among the Mahāsāṃghika school and been written in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> that was later turned into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, a claim that remains contested. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> it was copied often and cited by writers such as Vasubandhu, Candrakīrti and Śāntideva. According to Paramārtha there were over fifty Indian commentaries, but only one survives, and only in Chinese.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Dharmarakṣa's team made the first Chinese translation in 286 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>. It was superseded in 406 by the seven-fascicle translation of Kumārajīva's team, which became the standard version in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, although it lacks the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devadatta">Devadatta</a> chapter present in the earlier one. The sutra is the main scripture on which the Chinese Tiantai school and its derivatives, the Japanese Tendai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nichiren">Nichiren</a>, the Korean Cheontae and the Vietnamese Thiên Thai schools were founded. By the 8th century in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> the emperor had set up a network of nunneries in each province, the Temples for the Eradication of Sins through the Lotus.</p><p>It has shaped East Asian art and literature for over 1,400 years, and its scene of Shakyamuni and Prabhutaratna Buddhas seated together became a popular theme in Chinese Buddhist art. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matteo-ricci">Matteo Ricci</a> mentioned and denounced it in 1603. Eugène Burnouf completed a French translation in 1839, published in 1852.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lotus Sutra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Buddhism was encouraged across the empire, and local Chinese schools flourished: Zhiyi founded the Tiantai school and completed the Great Treatise on Concentration and Insight, teaching 'Three Thousand Realms in a Single Moment of Life' as the essence of the <b>Lotus Sutra</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mahayana produced a large new literature of sutras presented as the Buddha’s words, among them the Perfection of Wisdom sutras, including the Diamond Sutra and the Heart Sutra, the <b>Lotus Sutra</b> and the Pure Land sutras.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Tendai, brought from China by the monk Saichō, rested on the <b>Lotus Sutra</b> and taught that enlightenment was open to “every creature”; its monastery on Mount Hiei, above the capital, grew close to the court and taught great reverence for the emperor.</blockquote><a href="/societies/heian-japan">Social Hierarchy of Heian Japan</a></li><li><blockquote>According to one of his letters, the Japanese monk Nichiren lectured at Seicho-ji Temple on the supremacy of the <b>Lotus Sutra</b>, then first declared Namu Myōhō Renge Kyō in public atop Mount Kiyosumi.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-28">28 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A devout Buddhist, he brought the Kuchean monk Kumarajiva to Chang'an in 401 and gave him a translation bureau with hundreds of assistants; their versions of the <b>Lotus Sutra</b> and other texts became standard in East Asia.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-qin">Later Qin</a></li></ul></section>
